  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through Strongim Bisnis, expanded its climate resilience efforts in the tourism sector, recognising both its economic importance and vulnerability to climate risks. During the reporting period, Strongim Bisnis provided over AUD 313,000 in climate adaptation and resilience support, including investments in elevated jetties at Zipolo Habu and Fatboys Resorts, and facilitating seawall construction with several partners to protect against rising sea levels and extreme weather events. Partner-supported initiatives also promoted adaptive measures such as managed retreat&#x2014;relocating buildings further inland&#x2014;and constructing structures on stilts to reduce exposure to climate-related impacts.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">During the reporting period, Australia&#x2019;s Strongim Bisnis partnered with LG Marine, a woman-owned enterprise based in Gizo, Western Province, that aggregates and exports locally grown seaweed &#x2013; a sector of growing importance to the Solomon Islands economy. The partnership aimed to explore and develop additional premium international markets for exporting to. Over the reporting period, the program provided support to cover freight and handling costs for pilot shipments to new markets in Australia and the UK. As a result, LG Marine exported 134 tonnes of seaweed to first-time buyers, who paid an average premium of AUD5.74/kg above the standard price in other key markets &#x2014;generating more than AUD827,000 in additional export revenue. The seaweed was sourced from an existing network of 27 household growers in traditional seaweed-producing areas of Choiseul and Western Provinces, who in turn benefitted from increased prices for their produce.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strongim Bisnis, Australia&#x2019;s flagship private sector development program in Solomon Islands, improved access to training and advisory services for 453 smallholder farming households, geared towards strengthening their agricultural practices and building their resilience to external shocks such as climate change. Strongim Bisnis partnered with LF &amp; Sons, a cocoa aggregator in North Malaita to deliver agricultural extension trainings to its network of farmers. A total of 43 farmers, including 9 women, participated in training focused on grafting, pruning, and integrated pest and disease management. These practices help farmers adapt to climate-related challenges by promoting healthier, more productive trees and reducing crop losses from increasingly common pests and diseases. By strengthening farm management practices, the initiative supports long-term sustainability and improves the ability of farming households to withstand external shocks.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australia supported Strongim Bisnis Program partnered with a key national gender equality advocacy group, West Are&apos;Are Rokotanikeni Association Trust Board (WARA), an association of women&#x2019;s saving clubs across several provinces. Program contributions to partnership activities were spread over 3 years and focused on strengthening the associations governance systems, along with improving their associated savings club&#x2019;s capacity to offer financial services to their members- as well as gender sensitization activities within their communities.  As a result of this partnership, over the reporting period, 24 women entrepreneurs accessed SBD 25,600 (approximately AUD 4,670) worth of business activity related loans from their respective groups revolving loan fund facility.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian supported Strongim Bisnis program partnered with a major coconut value-addition processor, Islands Own Pty Ltd, to expand its commercial scale processing of coconuts into higher value coconut products (such as desiccated coconut, coconut cream, body care products, etc) targeted the local market. These value addition efforts seek to diversify the markets for Solomon Island coconut farmers from their current dependence on lower value copra production and exports.  Australian funding has focused on procurement of machinery to upgrade their coconut processing capacity, development of new labels and packaging for products targeting local markets, and incentives/support mechanisms to strengthen their coconut supply chain from household suppliers. Over the reporting period, 162 additional coconut farmers benefitted from improved market access as Islands Own expanded their coconut sourcing network to outer areas of Guadalcanal Province.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian-supported Strongim Bisnis program partnered with a large farmer association in Guadalcanal Province, the North-West Guadalcanal Development Association (NWGDA), to strengthen their 3200 registered member access to markets for their produce, and agricultural extension advice and inputs to improve their farming practices and yields. A key activity was the construction of a wholesale market facility near member communities. The facility provides members direct access to markets and buyers, thereby saving significant costs and time associated with transporting produce to Honiara city for sale at the central market. The program also facilitated a formal agreement between NWGDA and the largest food retailer in the country, Bulk Shop, to purchase locally grown produce from the market facility on a scheduled fortnightly basis. During the reporting period, 130 individual farmers (97 women and 33 men) from 16 communities sold their produce to Bulk Shop at this market.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through Strongim Bisnis, Australia partnered with the Solomon Islands Ministry of Commerce, Industry, Labour and Immigration to develop and launch the Iumi Gro [we grow] Business Accelerator program. Starting from 1 July 2025, ten selected entrepreneurs will undergo five months of intensive business training, along with coaching and mentoring provided by formal business development service providers. All participants have existing businesses and were selected from a national pool of applicants who responded to a public call for expressions of interest. Five of the participating entrepreneurs in the accelerator program are women.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
